**CALL  FOR  PAPERS**

15th International Conference on Formal Methods in Software Engineering (Fo=
rmaliSE 2027)
https://2027.formalise.org/

26=9627 April 2027
Dublin, Ireland

*Co-located with ICSE 2027*

SUBMISSION LINK: https://formalise27.hotcrp.com/u/0/

FormaliSE brings together the formal methods and software engineering commu=
nities to exchange ideas, experiences, techniques, and results, with the go=
al of fostering the development and application of formal methods that are =
both practically useful and capable of supporting high-quality software eng=
ineering.


**Topics of interest:**
We welcome contributions on all aspects of formal methods and software engi=
neering, including, but not limited to:
 - requirements formalization and formal specification;
 - verification and validation approaches, methods, and tools;
 - integration of formal methods into the software development lifecycle;
 - model-based engineering;
 - formal methods for AI-based systems (FM4AI) and AI applied to formal met=
hods (AI4FM);
 - synergies between LLMs/Agentic AI and formal methods;
 - correctness-by-construction approaches;
 - formal methods for safety, security, certification, and non-functional p=
roperties;
 - scalability and practical application of formal methods;
 - case studies, experience reports, guidelines, and usability of formal me=
thods.


**Submission categories:**
FormaliSE 2027 welcomes the following categories of contributions:
 - *Full Research Papers* (10 pages + 2 pages of references): describing or=
iginal research work and results.
 - *Experience Report Papers* (10 pages + 2 pages of references): discussin=
g a significant application that suggests general lessons learned and motiv=
ates further research, or empirically validates theoretical results
 - *Research Ideas Papers* (4 pages + 1 page of references): describing new=
 ideas in preliminary form with an early evaluation and including a plan fo=
r future studies
 - *Vision Papers* (5 pages + 1 page of references): outlining mid- and lon=
g-term visions or roadmaps on a (potentially controversial) topic relevant =
to the conference.
 - *Tool Demo Papers* (4 pages including references): describing novel tool=
s, outlining usage scenarios, including main screenshots, and a link to the=
 tool or a video demo.
 - *Posters* (2 pages including references): describing work in progress.


Submissions must follow the IEEE conference proceedings format.

All papers submitted to the FormaliSE 2027 conference must be written in En=
glish, double-blind, must be unpublished original work, and must not be und=
er review or submitted elsewhere at the time of submission.

The official publication date is the date the proceedings are made availabl=
e in the ACM or IEEE Digital Libraries. This date may be up to two weeks pr=
ior to the first day of ICSE 2027. The official publication date affects th=
e deadline for any patent filings related to published work.

Purchases of additional pages in the proceedings is not allowed.

**Artifact Evaluation:**
FormaliSE 2027 continues its Artifact Evaluation (AE) process to promote re=
producibility, transparency, and reuse of research results. Authors are enc=
ouraged to submit supporting artifacts such as software, datasets, models, =
proofs, or other materials that substantiate the claims made in their paper=
s.

Artifact submission is optional, but strongly encouraged whenever applicabl=
e. Artifacts will be evaluated separately with respect to their consistency=
, completeness, documentation, and ease of use. Successfully evaluated arti=
facts will be awarded the EAPLS Artifact Badges that apply (among =93Functi=
onal=94, =93Reusable=94, and =93Available=94)

**Important Dates (AoE):**
 - 30 October 2026 =96 Abstract submission
 - 6 November 2026 =96 Paper submission
 - 10 November 2026 =96 Artifact submission
 - 11 January 2027 =96 Paper notification
 - 15 January 2027 =96 Artifact notification
 - 29 January 2027 =96 Camera-ready submission


